//! SOS kernel core (designs 112, 140, 162) — the arch-free kernel.
//!
//! Everything a SOS kernel image shares: the console, the trap handler `ktrap`,
//! the object-op dispatch, and the sosimg loader. An image is one per-arch
//! kernel HAL (`sos/hal/<arch>/kernel/`) + the shared runtime seams
//! (`sosrt`, sos/rt/common/src/lib.saw) + this module + one entry `.saw` defining
//! `kmain` — `sos/kernel/main.saw` for the real kernel, `sos/tests/*.saw` for
//! the harness cases. Sharing the module is what keeps every test image on the
//! SAME trap path instead of a copy of it.
//!
//! NO ARCHITECTURE APPEARS IN THIS DIRECTORY (design 162 unit 1). Registers,
//! trap causes, protection hardware, the board's console and the way the machine
//! stops are all reached through `hal`, and the harness enforces it: a scan
//! rejects an arch name anywhere under `core/`, so the seam cannot erode by one
//! convenient constant at a time. Adding an architecture is writing a HAL.
//!
//! Addresses and machine words are `UInt` throughout, never `Int` — `Int` is
//! platform-width and SIGNED, so a RAM address lands in its negative half on a
//! 32-bit profile and means a different number on a 64-bit one (DF-140a).
//!
//! # THIS FILE IS THE FACADE (the Aug-17 split)
//!
//! The kernel used to be one 4000-line file. It is now fourteen, one per seam,
//! and this file holds no code: it re-exports the names an image entry uses, so
//! `import kcore.{console, load_root_and_enter}` in `main.saw` and in every
//! `sos/tests/` entry means exactly what it always did.
//!
//! THE MODULES, IN DEPENDENCY ORDER — a file may import only those above it,
//! because an import cycle is not diagnosed (DF-232e); it silently empties a
//! module's export table and blames a third file:
//!
//!   `limits`     every slab size, the frame stride, the "no slot" sentinels
//!   `mem`        addresses and byte moves
//!   `diag`       the console, and the reports that stop the machine
//!   `result`     a syscall's answer, and the one place it enters a frame
//!   `objects`    the handle table, the validated per-kind objects, the Memory
//!                slab and the boot-handle queue
//!   `threads`    the thread slab, the frame arena, the ready queue
//!   `time`       the Clock/Timer slabs and the one hardware comparator
//!   `process`    the process slab, the grant record, the copy doors, the way
//!                into user mode
//!   `waitables`  the Event/Waiter/Interrupt slabs and the per-kind matrix
//!   `refs`       the reference count, free-on-zero, the per-kind last rites
//!                (sawos design 7), AND the delivery that turns readiness into
//!                a woken thread (`wake` merged in at M4 unit 3 — see below)
//!   `irq`        the tick, expiry, device lines, the idle path, the point
//!   `preempt`    the strided long-op movers that take that point
//!   `loader`     the sosimg loader — validate, place, record
//!   `sched`      the switch point, and the end of a process
//!   `dispatch`   every object op, and `ktrap`
//!
//! TWO OF THOSE MOVED IN M3 UNIT 2 (sawos design 2), and both moves are the
//! same rule the `preempt` split below states — a module sits at the altitude
//! its CALL GRAPH allows, and where a piece lives is decided by what it has to
//! reach:
//!
//!   - the TEARDOWN left `process` for `sched`. Ending a process used to stop
//!     the machine; with more than one it RESCHEDULES, and a reschedule can
//!     idle, and idling delivers, and delivery reaches back down into
//!     `process`'s own copy door. Written in `process` it would be the cycle
//!     DF-232e diagnoses. `sched.saw`'s header has the full argument; its
//!     visible cost is that the copy doors REPORT a bad buffer and their callers
//!     terminate on it.
//!   - the LOADER left the top for just below `sched`. It was last because it
//!     ended by handing the machine to `start_process`; it now has a SECOND
//!     door — `process_create`, which is an op — so its phases have to be
//!     reachable from `dispatch`. It reaches nothing above itself either way.
//!
//! `preempt` is where it is because of design 1's one structural finding: a
//! preemption point DELIVERS, so it sits above `irq`, while the byte loops it
//! drives sit at the bottom under `mem` — delivery reaches them through
//! `refs` -> `process` -> `copy_out`. A point written inside the loop would be
//! `mem` importing `irq`, which is the cycle this list exists to prevent. So the
//! loop stays a primitive, the point stays beside the idle poll, and the cadence
//! that joins them is its own module between them. `preempt.saw`'s header has
//! the full argument.
//!
//! ONE MORE MOVED IN M3 UNIT 5.5 (sawos design 8), and it is the same rule
//! again: `waitables` PASSED `process`, where the list above used to have it
//! one line earlier. A Process handle became §2.2's fourth waitable, so the
//! per-kind matrix's Process arms read `PROCESSES` — who is watching this
//! slot, is it `Gone`, what §8 word does its record carry — and a module that
//! reads the process table sits above the module that owns it. Nothing went
//! the other way: `process` names nothing in `waitables`, which is what makes
//! this a reordering rather than the cycle DF-232e diagnoses.
//!
//! AND ONE MODULE STOPPED EXISTING IN M4 UNIT 3 (sawos design 15), which is the
//! same rule taken to its conclusion: `wake` — delivery — MERGED INTO `refs`,
//! because the two became mutually recursive. `designs/010` ruling 9(b) makes a
//! DELIVERY drop a counted reference (a one-shot attachment detaches when its
//! record is copied out) and the peer-gone terminal levels make a DROPPED
//! REFERENCE deliver (a column reaching zero is what raises them, and
//! `drop_reference` is the only place that is observed). Two modules cannot
//! hold that, and DF-232e does not diagnose the cycle — it empties an export
//! table and blames a third file — so the two seams became one. `refs.saw`'s
//! header carries the full argument.
//!
//! WHERE THE LINE FELL, when a seam of the sketch came in two: the STATE of a
//! thing sits below the process teardown that frees it, and ACTING on it sits
//! above — because ending a process touches every slab, and answering a wait
//! goes through the copy door that can end one. That is why a thread's table
//! (`threads`) is not its scheduler (`sched`), a timer's schedule (`time`) is
//! not its expiry (`irq`), and the wait tables (`waitables`) are not the
//! delivery (`refs`' second half, which was `wake` until M4 unit 3 and is the
//! same altitude under a different file name). The unit-5.5 move above does not bend that rule: the
//! process SLAB and the wait tables are both STATE, so their order relative to
//! each other was never fixed by it — only their order relative to the
//! teardown was, and both are still below it.
//!
//! WHAT A SPLIT COSTS HERE (DF-232f): Saw has no package-internal visibility, so
//! every name two seams share is `public` — visible to anything that imports
//! `kcore`, not merely to its siblings. Design 80's gate no longer protects the
//! kernel's own tables from the kernel's own consumers. Nothing about the IMAGE
//! changed; the widening is a compile-time fact, and it is the argument for an
//! `internal` tier rather than a reason to keep one 4000-line file.

public import kcore.mem.{align_up}
public import kcore.diag.{Console, ExitCode, console, fatal, fatal_image}
public import kcore.time.{start_tick}
public import kcore.process.{start_process}
public import kcore.irq.{timer_ticks}
public import kcore.preempt.{long_copy, long_zero}
public import kcore.dispatch.{ktrap}
public import kcore.loader.{load_root_and_enter}
